<div class="option-grid">
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-triple">Keep the triple lock</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The highest of prices, earnings and 2.5%.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>It rises at least as fast as prices and as earnings, every year. Over time it tends to rise faster than both, when the measure that wins changes from year to year. The level in future is uncertain. The Institute for Fiscal Studies gives <q>{{ ("ifs-range-2050" | claim).passage }}</q>. {{ m.c("ifs-range-2050") }}</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Current pensioners, and future ones more so, because increases build up over time. {{ m.c("ppi-future-workers") }} People who live longer gain more. {{ m.c("ifs-longevity") }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>The highest cost of the rules in the calculator, apart from small differences caused by rounding, and the least predictable.</dd>
      <dt>A judgement, not a finding</dt> <dd>Whether the pension should rise as a share of earnings, and how far.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-double">Double lock: prices or earnings</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The higher of prices and earnings. The 2.5% minimum is removed.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>The same increase as the triple lock in any year when prices or earnings rise by 2.5% or more, though the amount can stay lower because of earlier years. A smaller increase in other years. Over time it tends to rise faster than both prices and earnings, when the higher of the two changes from year to year.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>The same people, by less. Modelling from 2018 put the effect on pensioner poverty in 2050 at {{ "ppi-poverty-double-lock" | figShown }} more people than under the triple lock. {{ m.ref(("ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figure).source) }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>{{ ("no-costed-double-lock" | claim).statement }}</dd>
      <dt>A precedent</dt> <dd>{{ ("double-lock-2017" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("double-lock-2017") }}</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-earnings">Earnings link</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The rise in average earnings. This is the minimum the law already requires.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>It keeps its value compared with pay. In a year when prices rise faster than pay, it loses buying power.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Modelling from 2018 put the number of extra pensioners in poverty in 2050 at {{ "ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figShown }}, compared with the triple lock. {{ m.sourceNote("ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figure) }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>Small savings at first, large over decades. See <a href="/reform-options/costs/#cost-and-saving">what a change would save</a>.</dd>
      <dt>A judgement, not a finding</dt> <dd>Whether a year of lost buying power is acceptable if the pension keeps pace with pay over time.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-prices">Prices link</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The rise in the Consumer Prices Index.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>It keeps its buying power against the official index. When pay rises faster than prices, which is the usual long-run pattern, it falls further behind pay each year.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Future pensioners most, because the gap with earnings grows with time.</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>Usually the largest saving of the rules in the calculator, when earnings grow faster than prices. The order depends on the figures for each year. See <a href="/reform-options/costs/#cost-and-saving">what a change would save</a>.</dd>
      <dt>The law</dt> <dd>It would pay less than the present legal minimum in most years, so the law would have to change. From 1980 to 2010 the law required only a prices link. {{ m.c("earnings-link-1980") }}</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
</div>

